1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,200
Where secret homes and hideaways meet survival of the fittest.

2
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:06,700
Animal builders that claw their way to the top.

3
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:09,200
And sink their teeth into their work.

4
00:00:09,900 --> 00:00:13,000
All to make a little place they can call home.

5
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:17,200
Now, Nature's Engineers on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:31,140 --> 00:00:38,880
Mighty dams that hold back the wildest of rivers, intricate tunnels that connect entire communities, and towering skyscrapers buzzing with life.

7
00:00:38,881 --> 00:00:45,840
This is construction animal style, where every day the world is remade by creatures seemingly

8
00:00:45,841 --> 00:00:48,760
designed to alter their habitat.

9
00:00:48,761 --> 00:00:50,760
We call them nature's engineers.

10
00:00:50,761 --> 00:00:57,260
The way most animals that build things do it is by running through a series of routines.

11
00:00:57,261 --> 00:01:02,180
What they do is the same thing over and over and over again until they reach some criterion,

12
00:01:02,181 --> 00:01:04,340
which they recognize innately.

13
00:01:04,341 --> 00:01:12,100
Then they switch to the next routine and they run through it over and over again.

14
00:01:12,101 --> 00:01:15,780
Dam building is one of humanity's earliest triumphs.

15
00:01:15,781 --> 00:01:20,220
Our ancestors' ability to create reservoirs that held precious water was paramount for

16
00:01:20,221 --> 00:01:22,220
early agriculture.

17
00:01:22,221 --> 00:01:27,780
Later, humans began to understand the power generating potential in holding back water

18
00:01:27,781 --> 00:01:31,560
and slowly releasing it to drive water wheels.

19
00:01:31,561 --> 00:01:36,060
Through millennia, the art and science of dam building has been perfected with such

20
00:01:36,061 --> 00:01:42,120
massive water stopping and power generating structures as the Hoover Dam.

21
00:01:42,121 --> 00:01:47,520
But long before the first human industry tried to block even a tiny stream, an animal craftsman

22
00:01:47,521 --> 00:01:51,660
was fashioning dams of great size and sophistication.

23
00:01:51,661 --> 00:01:57,360
One of my favorite animals is beaver, with the exception of corals, building huge coral

24
00:01:57,361 --> 00:02:02,400
reefs, they create the largest structures that are made by animals, sometimes a quarter

25
00:02:02,401 --> 00:02:07,460
mile across, sufficiently well made that you could ride a horse across the dam, for example.

26
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:13,600
Like most of nature's engineers, the beaver's purpose is to construct a secure home to raise

27
00:02:13,601 --> 00:02:16,259
its young and stay safe from predators.

28
00:02:16,260 --> 00:02:19,600
For a pair of mating beavers, this means building

29
00:02:19,601 --> 00:02:21,346
a sturdy home called a lodge.

30
00:02:21,347 --> 00:02:25,080
But the animals are very particular when it comes to location.

31
00:02:25,920 --> 00:02:29,280
For added protection, they prefer their home be surrounded by water.

32
00:02:30,540 --> 00:02:33,980
For many, the first step in building a house is building a lake,

33
00:02:34,480 --> 00:02:38,060
and that involves creating one of nature's most sizable engineering feats,

34
00:02:38,760 --> 00:02:39,480
the beaver dam.

35
00:02:41,940 --> 00:02:45,580
But even the largest structures must begin with the felling of a single tree.

36
00:02:50,820 --> 00:02:55,040
Down here near the water, we have a prime example of beaver activity.

37
00:02:55,080 --> 00:03:06,740
You see down on the base, the beaver's gotten in here, chiseled away with those chisel-type teeth, gotten into the meat of the tree or the cambion layer.

38
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:12,140
If a beaver takes down this willow, its likely use would be for dam building or for forage.

39
00:03:12,580 --> 00:03:21,520
And if the beaver's going to forage off the tree, it would likely use some of the limbs up top that are more easily digestible, the bark's thinner.

40
00:03:21,521 --> 00:03:27,340
If the beaver uses this for dam building, once the tree is down, it's probably going to strip off some of the branches.

41
00:03:31,060 --> 00:03:35,180
For humans, even with the proper tools, felling a tree is hard work.

42
00:03:36,420 --> 00:03:41,960
But beavers are well adapted for this task, and the only tools they need are right in their own skulls.

43
00:03:42,360 --> 00:03:47,060
Here's a beaver skull, and you can see that the front two teeth are very sharp.

44
00:03:47,340 --> 00:03:51,340
There's a layer of hard enamel on the outside and soft dentine.

45
00:03:51,520 --> 00:03:55,120
and they work together, and when they touch, they sharpen each other.

46
00:03:55,540 --> 00:04:00,460
This tooth extends almost this far into the lower jaw, likewise in the upper jaw.

47
00:04:00,461 --> 00:04:04,780
They have extremely powerful muscles that operate those jaws.

48
00:04:05,260 --> 00:04:10,120
Those masseter muscles that attach here to bite through that wood are very, very strong.

49
00:04:10,940 --> 00:04:14,840
The rest of the beaver's body is equally well designed for aquatic construction.

50
00:04:15,840 --> 00:04:20,160
Their small, agile front feet let them weave branches and scoop mud.

51
00:04:20,161 --> 00:04:22,480
two very important steps in dam building.

52
00:04:24,180 --> 00:04:27,060
In contrast to the popularly held cartoon image,

53
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:31,420
beavers do not use their broad, flat tails to shovel and pack mud.

54
00:04:32,400 --> 00:04:34,440
Instead, they use them as rudders when swimming.

55
00:04:35,260 --> 00:04:36,460
And if the animal is startled,

56
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:39,920
they slap their tails against the water to sound an alarm.

57
00:04:42,120 --> 00:04:46,340
The beaver's webbed rear feet also allow them to move effortlessly through the water.

58
00:04:47,180 --> 00:04:50,080
Which is important, because if you're going to build a dam,

59
00:04:50,160 --> 00:04:51,580
You have to get your feet wet.

60
00:04:52,540 --> 00:04:56,260
The beavers will start chewing off small twigs and branches off of trees

61
00:04:56,261 --> 00:05:00,440
and plugging them into areas where they can get a foothold in the bottom of the stream

62
00:05:00,441 --> 00:05:04,100
so that as they pack mud and more sticks and bigger sticks behind them,

63
00:05:04,420 --> 00:05:05,660
the water starts to build up.

64
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:11,340
Where rising water behind an unfinished dam would spell disaster to human engineers,

65
00:05:12,380 --> 00:05:15,320
beavers seem to recognize and exploit the construction advantages.

66
00:05:16,380 --> 00:05:18,820
Beaver are built to deal with water.

67
00:05:18,821 --> 00:05:23,260
And when they are building their dam, they take use of the fact that wood floats.

68
00:05:23,900 --> 00:05:26,320
They harvest stuff around the dam.

69
00:05:26,760 --> 00:05:33,580
If they have to, they will build channels way out, long ways out, deep channels, 18 inches deep, that will fill with water.

70
00:05:34,020 --> 00:05:36,020
They'll drop the trees, they'll pull them in.

71
00:05:36,460 --> 00:05:39,580
It's like a barge dragging the stuff using the water.

72
00:05:39,900 --> 00:05:42,040
So a lot of the material comes from upstream.

73
00:05:43,440 --> 00:05:48,020
The final step for most beavers is the packing of stones and mud between the loose wood.

74
00:05:48,820 --> 00:05:55,340
The exterior coating helps to solidify the structure and keeps water from seeping through small cracks and fissures.

75
00:05:56,440 --> 00:05:58,540
But the beaver's job is never really finished.

76
00:05:59,360 --> 00:06:02,680
The animal will continue to maintain the structure from season to season.

77
00:06:03,420 --> 00:06:09,400
Beaver don't have any need to keep their dams as absolute zero-flow devices.

78
00:06:10,020 --> 00:06:16,060
Although during much of the year they'll busily repair any damage, find any leaks, and patch them.

79
00:06:16,061 --> 00:06:21,400
In other parts of the year, when there's a lot of stream flow, they may actually lower the dam,

80
00:06:21,540 --> 00:06:24,900
provide a spillway to take strain off of the dam.

81
00:06:25,220 --> 00:06:28,360
And during the winter, they'll wait until the ice is formed,

82
00:06:28,480 --> 00:06:31,820
and then they'll go and actually cut a hole just below the ice through the dam

83
00:06:31,821 --> 00:06:37,760
and lower the water level slightly so that they have an airspace to breathe in between the water and the ice.

84
00:06:40,840 --> 00:06:45,200
But for all its fame, the Beaver Dam is only a means to an end.

85
00:06:46,060 --> 00:06:51,200
The beaver dam is the most obvious structure in the beaver's habitat,

86
00:06:51,540 --> 00:06:55,840
but the pond behind the beaver dam is really where the beaver lives and where it thrives.

87
00:06:56,060 --> 00:06:57,120
The lodge is built there.

88
00:06:57,640 --> 00:07:00,960
That provides the animal protection from predators and from the elements.

89
00:07:01,400 --> 00:07:03,380
These lodges are really fascinating.

90
00:07:03,900 --> 00:07:06,960
When I was growing up, I thought that when a beaver made a lodge,

91
00:07:06,961 --> 00:07:10,540
it actually built a hole in there as it was building up,

92
00:07:10,620 --> 00:07:13,340
and when it completed the structure, it would have the hole in there.

93
00:07:13,740 --> 00:07:14,480
But they don't.

94
00:07:14,481 --> 00:07:27,740
They just pack stuff, logs and branches and mud, and then when they're done, they go from outside underneath with their claws and with their teeth,

95
00:07:27,840 --> 00:07:33,240
and they dig away at the packed structure that they've made and hollow out their lodge.

96
00:07:33,800 --> 00:07:36,100
The inside of the lodge isn't all that big.

97
00:07:36,720 --> 00:07:41,440
You know, this one is probably 12 feet across and five or so feet tall,

98
00:07:41,441 --> 00:07:45,300
but the inside is only a few feet across and a few feet tall.

99
00:07:45,960 --> 00:07:48,020
Most beaver lodges are two-story affairs.

100
00:07:49,160 --> 00:07:52,160
The first story, or chamber, is closest to the entrance

101
00:07:52,161 --> 00:07:55,320
and is used by the animal to dry off upon returning home.

102
00:07:56,460 --> 00:08:00,360
The second is where the animals spend most of their time huddled together for warmth.

103
00:08:03,740 --> 00:08:06,140
The beavers take great care when constructing their homes

104
00:08:06,141 --> 00:08:09,240
because they often plan on staying a long time.

105
00:08:10,020 --> 00:08:13,120
The beaver will add to the lodge every year.

106
00:08:13,660 --> 00:08:18,140
As you can see, it was mainly a mud and small twig lodge,

107
00:08:18,280 --> 00:08:20,420
and it's dragged up some pretty big pieces.

108
00:08:21,120 --> 00:08:24,580
Later on, if it goes out further or if the pond becomes deeper

109
00:08:24,581 --> 00:08:28,500
and it has access to even larger trees, it will tote some of those across.

110
00:08:28,940 --> 00:08:31,700
On some of the lakes around here that have beaver lodges,

111
00:08:31,701 --> 00:08:35,180
some of the logs that are brought up are six or so inches in diameter.

112
00:08:36,180 --> 00:08:40,420
In the end, here is where the beavers' months of hard work pays off.

113
00:08:41,560 --> 00:08:43,360
Protected from the elements and predators,

114
00:08:43,900 --> 00:08:47,420
the pair welcomes a new generation of baby builders to the lodge.

115
00:08:48,580 --> 00:08:50,300
Beavers are incredible engineers.

116
00:08:50,840 --> 00:08:52,980
They may not really understand what they're building,

117
00:08:53,340 --> 00:08:55,240
but the results of what they create,

118
00:08:55,241 --> 00:09:00,480
the huge lodge, the large dams, have a profound effect on the ecosystem.

119
00:09:01,080 --> 00:09:04,980
In this situation, they built a dam on the outlet of a lake.

120
00:09:05,180 --> 00:09:09,540
and they raised the water level of this lake almost three feet.

121
00:09:10,100 --> 00:09:16,020
A 40 or 50 acre lake, and that's 350 acre feet of water,

122
00:09:16,620 --> 00:09:24,740
which is an amazing amount of water to be backed up behind a pile of sticks and mud and rock.

123
00:09:24,880 --> 00:09:27,940
The dam's inelegance is the secret to its strength.

124
00:09:28,760 --> 00:09:31,880
But not all animals share the same crude design philosophy.

125
00:09:31,881 --> 00:09:41,930
Beavers may be some of the best known builders on the planet, but their wooden creations look simple compared to the complex structures built by some of nature's most

126
00:09:41,931 --> 00:09:43,080
exacting engineers.

127
00:09:44,460 --> 00:09:52,780
The largest beaver dam on record was located in Three Forks, Montana and measured over 2,100 feet long.

128
00:09:53,680 --> 00:09:56,380
Nature's Engineers will return on Modern Marvels.

129
00:09:56,381 --> 00:10:03,220
We now return to Nature's Engineers on Modern Marbles.

130
00:10:04,020 --> 00:10:07,200
With six sides intersecting at 60-degree angles,

131
00:10:07,980 --> 00:10:11,320
the hexagon is one of nature's strongest and most efficient shapes.

132
00:10:11,980 --> 00:10:16,420
The hexagon is the most efficient structure that can be built.

133
00:10:16,700 --> 00:10:21,600
You have the maximum volume in the cell with the minimum structure on the outside,

134
00:10:21,860 --> 00:10:23,760
and each one fits perfectly to the next.

135
00:10:23,761 --> 00:10:30,320
In the 1940s, the visionary Buckminster Fuller recognized the hexagon's potential in construction.

136
00:10:31,180 --> 00:10:35,400
The flower of his work was the space-age structure known as a geodesic dome,

137
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:39,040
which offered stability and a lot of interior space.

138
00:10:41,260 --> 00:10:45,120
Hexagon-shaped materials are ideal whenever a structure must be light and strong.

139
00:10:46,620 --> 00:10:49,500
Today, they can be found just under the skin of jet fighters,

140
00:10:49,501 --> 00:10:53,040
and even on the space shuttle.

141
00:10:53,760 --> 00:10:59,600
But long before the hexagon was protecting astronauts and orbiting high above the Earth,

142
00:11:00,060 --> 00:11:03,460
it could be found in a field and usually held something sweet.

143
00:11:04,500 --> 00:11:08,500
Honeybees build combs that have hexagonal cells in them.

144
00:11:08,880 --> 00:11:12,220
This turns out to be one of the structurally strongest forms that they could do.

145
00:11:12,680 --> 00:11:16,800
And it probably has more to do with actually how the cells fit together

146
00:11:16,801 --> 00:11:19,580
than intentionally making them to be strong.

147
00:11:19,581 --> 00:11:23,840
but it works out that they can make very large structures that are very light in weight.

148
00:11:24,980 --> 00:11:28,840
Honeybees live throughout the world in all but the very coldest climates.

149
00:11:29,840 --> 00:11:32,580
A buzzing, roiling hive is an animal metropolis

150
00:11:32,581 --> 00:11:37,800
held together by careful engineering, communal living, and some very sticky material.

151
00:11:40,040 --> 00:11:44,300
Scientists who study these animals take great care not to get on their subject's bad side.

152
00:11:44,980 --> 00:11:46,540
I'm attempting to light a smoker

153
00:11:46,541 --> 00:11:50,660
because if you puff smoke into the colony, you don't get met with as much resistance.

154
00:11:51,480 --> 00:11:56,720
We have to use a hive tool to break in because the honeybees collect plant resins, pine pitch,

155
00:11:56,880 --> 00:11:58,960
and they use that to seal every single crack.

156
00:11:59,060 --> 00:12:02,780
So every comb is stuck in, every box is stuck to the other box the cover is stuck on,

157
00:12:02,860 --> 00:12:03,760
so we have to break in.

158
00:12:04,280 --> 00:12:09,420
Each hive is ruled by a queen, and when new queens are born, only one can stay.

159
00:12:10,820 --> 00:12:14,440
The honeycomb beehive takes shape when one of the queens, like this one,

160
00:12:14,441 --> 00:12:16,860
marked by the beekeeper with a yellow dot.

161
00:12:17,900 --> 00:12:21,900
And a portion of the worker bees leave a functioning hive to build a new home.

162
00:12:22,480 --> 00:12:26,080
The swarming insects look for knots in trees, clefts in rocks,

163
00:12:26,620 --> 00:12:28,340
or abandoned burrows in the ground.

164
00:12:28,660 --> 00:12:32,680
But on occasion, confused bees have been known to start building on top of cars.

165
00:12:34,560 --> 00:12:36,480
For many of these winged engineers,

166
00:12:36,940 --> 00:12:40,680
the best man-made structures are those built specifically for them.

167
00:12:40,681 --> 00:12:46,800
They already know that I'm here, and the reason that I can tell you that is they can feel vibrations,

168
00:12:47,260 --> 00:12:50,020
and so they are actually aware that something has come up,

169
00:12:50,100 --> 00:12:53,280
but these bees are pretty docile, so it's not that much of a problem.

170
00:12:53,820 --> 00:12:55,100
We put some smoke in the entrance.

171
00:12:55,720 --> 00:12:56,500
We can look around.

172
00:12:56,600 --> 00:12:59,540
If there's another place out here where there's a hole in there, using that as an entrance,

173
00:12:59,620 --> 00:13:02,620
we'd have to smoke that too because that's where the guard bees are, but I don't see any.

174
00:13:03,140 --> 00:13:07,320
A mature, functioning hive is a wonder of cooperation and organization.

175
00:13:07,321 --> 00:13:21,300
Over the course of their lives, each bee will perform nearly every task in the hive, including foraging, guard duty, and even babysitting.

176
00:13:22,200 --> 00:13:26,800
As young workers, the bees spend time looking after the larvae developing in the brood cone.

177
00:13:28,140 --> 00:13:35,820
But the most important stage in a bee's life, for construction, is when they begin to produce their own unique and pliant building material.

178
00:13:37,320 --> 00:13:43,049
wax. The substance is formed by glands inside the bee's body.

179
00:13:43,050 --> 00:13:45,960
Once secreted, the wax collects

180
00:13:45,961 --> 00:13:50,122
in thin sheets between the plates of the insect's abdomen.

181
00:13:50,123 --> 00:13:52,920
Those glands usually get activated when

182
00:13:52,921 --> 00:13:57,944
the bee is fully engorged with nectar. This occurs in two principal circumstances.

183
00:13:57,945 --> 00:13:58,740
One is if the

184
00:13:58,741 --> 00:14:03,500
colony is bringing in lots of nectar from the outside, having a hard time finding a place to

185
00:14:03,501 --> 00:14:04,201
store it.

186
00:14:04,202 --> 00:14:08,900
Another circumstance is when a colony swarms, the individuals in the swarm, before

187
00:14:08,901 --> 00:14:12,150
they leave the hive, fill up their crops with honey.

188
00:14:12,151 --> 00:14:14,400
The bees chew the newly manufactured

189
00:14:14,401 --> 00:14:18,735
wax to make it malleable and seem to place it entirely by feel.

190
00:14:18,736 --> 00:14:20,180
How do the bees build

191
00:14:20,181 --> 00:14:21,845
these perfect hexagons?

192
00:14:21,846 --> 00:14:26,260
We don't know all of the answer yet, but one of the things we

193
00:14:26,261 --> 00:14:31,120
do know is that the size of them and presumably the angles between them are sensed by the

194
00:14:31,121 --> 00:14:32,863
essentially with her forelegs.

195
00:14:32,864 --> 00:14:36,640
So if the legs of a bee are damaged, she will build an exact comb

196
00:14:36,641 --> 00:14:38,941
relative to a bee with intact legs.

197
00:14:38,942 --> 00:14:43,280
For scientists, other questions arise, such as how do all the bees

198
00:14:43,281 --> 00:14:45,794
know in which direction to build?

199
00:14:45,795 --> 00:14:50,440
Bees appear to use magnetic fields to help them line up those

200
00:14:50,441 --> 00:14:57,280
combs parallel to each other, so that when a colony moves into a new cavity, the orientation of the

201
00:14:57,281 --> 00:15:02,320
combs that they tend to build has been shown to be correlated with the orientation of the combs

202
00:15:02,321 --> 00:15:05,350
in their previous hive, in the previous nest.

203
00:15:05,351 --> 00:15:08,380
And if an experimenter experimentally removes

204
00:15:08,381 --> 00:15:13,840
the earth's magnetic field by using electromagnetic coils, that consistency is lost.

205
00:15:14,220 --> 00:15:21,020
In the end, all the cells will be built almost exactly the same, with between 25 and 30 cells

206
00:15:21,021 --> 00:15:22,202
per square inch.

207
00:15:22,203 --> 00:15:27,520
The bees' construction is so uniform that beekeepers are able to install

208
00:15:27,521 --> 00:15:34,179
man-made foundation, special wax sheets with molded hexagons, into their box hives.

209
00:15:34,180 --> 00:15:34,880
The

210
00:15:34,881 --> 00:15:41,050
bees build directly onto the foundation, and the result is honeycomb in a box.

211
00:15:41,051 --> 00:15:41,900
The length

212
00:15:41,901 --> 00:15:47,820
of the cells vary depending on species and intended use. Honeycomb cells can be some

213
00:15:47,821 --> 00:15:53,100
of the longest, while brood comb cells built to house the queen's eggs are shorter, usually

214
00:15:53,101 --> 00:15:55,260
just big enough to house the growing larvae.

215
00:15:55,261 --> 00:16:02,220
The cells are adjusted seasonally depending on the kind of individuals they want to raise.

216
00:16:02,221 --> 00:16:07,780
Queens require a larger cell, so when they want to turn a worker larva into a queen,

217
00:16:07,781 --> 00:16:13,020
they actually lengthen the cell by half again probably and give it much more food and special

218
00:16:13,021 --> 00:16:14,020
nutrients.

219
00:16:14,021 --> 00:16:19,300
The queen bee seems to inspect each cell in the brood comb before depositing one of the

220
00:16:19,301 --> 00:16:22,500
over half a million eggs she will lay during her lifetime.

221
00:16:22,501 --> 00:16:26,700
If any little heads come up, use a little bit more smoke.

222
00:16:26,701 --> 00:16:33,480
Okay, in this particular comb we have mostly capped brood, which is the suede looking material

223
00:16:33,481 --> 00:16:35,860
that's a little reddish in color.

224
00:16:35,861 --> 00:16:38,500
And then out here by my right thumb, this is honey.

225
00:16:38,501 --> 00:16:41,380
You can see that the cappings are a little bit shinier.

226
00:16:41,381 --> 00:16:46,940
There's a very small amount of pollen right along here and also along here.

227
00:16:47,340 --> 00:16:52,120
So this is the classic honey over the top, pollen along the edges,

228
00:16:52,740 --> 00:16:57,060
brewed in the middle comb, like you would see a picture of in a textbook somewhere.

229
00:16:57,840 --> 00:17:03,380
But survival of the species demands that bees, even in the perfect home, must eventually leave.

230
00:17:04,300 --> 00:17:09,080
From old hives come new swarms, which fly off to make a new home

231
00:17:09,081 --> 00:17:12,480
and start the entire geometric construction process again.

232
00:17:17,460 --> 00:17:21,600
But bees aren't the only insect city builders to make use of the hexagon.

233
00:17:22,540 --> 00:17:25,880
Wasps have a similar construction style to house their offspring.

234
00:17:27,300 --> 00:17:31,260
Starting with a strong branch, the wasps build a bell-shaped comb,

235
00:17:31,800 --> 00:17:34,400
with each cell large enough to fit a wasp larva.

236
00:17:35,180 --> 00:17:41,380
Paper wasps make their colonies, and they actually do have hexagonal cells in those, just like the honeybees do.

237
00:17:41,980 --> 00:17:49,780
But these insects don't produce wax, and so they construct their colonies out of plant fibers that they collect by scraping fibers off leaves.

238
00:17:49,781 --> 00:17:53,820
They mix that with saliva, and then they build the paper using that material.

239
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:58,600
So it's a different kind of construction, but sort of similar engineering principles.

240
00:17:59,120 --> 00:18:00,780
Just as with the honeybee's creation,

241
00:18:01,800 --> 00:18:05,880
the hexagonal shape allows all the cells to fit together in a modular fashion.

242
00:18:07,040 --> 00:18:11,100
Once finished with a group of cells, many wasps construct additional combs

243
00:18:11,101 --> 00:18:13,520
and stack them like floors in a hotel.

244
00:18:14,520 --> 00:18:18,840
That material is supplied in a series of layers with air spaces between it.

245
00:18:18,841 --> 00:18:21,680
It provides good insulation to help keep the colonies warm.

246
00:18:22,320 --> 00:18:24,860
Wasp colonies are sometimes built out in the open

247
00:18:24,861 --> 00:18:29,400
as sort of football-shaped nests that you might see hanging in trees.

248
00:18:29,780 --> 00:18:32,620
Other times they're built inside of cavities in the ground.

249
00:18:33,380 --> 00:18:35,620
But no matter where wasps build,

250
00:18:36,140 --> 00:18:38,440
their survival depends on the strength of their homes.

251
00:18:39,500 --> 00:18:42,220
Fortunately, their nests are unexpectedly tough.

252
00:18:43,020 --> 00:18:45,600
Actually, the paper they make is pretty waterproof.

253
00:18:46,740 --> 00:18:50,100
It's something in the saliva that they mix with the plant fibers.

254
00:18:50,101 --> 00:18:52,960
It makes it quite strong, very lightweight.

255
00:18:52,961 --> 00:18:56,760
But typically, the nest will only be used for one season.

256
00:18:58,120 --> 00:19:00,500
The wasps, like all animals, are practical engineers.

257
00:19:01,580 --> 00:19:03,580
Overbuilding a structure means extra work.

258
00:19:04,220 --> 00:19:09,260
And in the animal world, that equates to extra food, a luxury that's not always available.

259
00:19:10,760 --> 00:19:16,860
But on rare occasions, when all the animal's needs are met, the nests can take on a life of their own.

260
00:19:16,861 --> 00:19:25,020
The largest wasp nest I've ever seen was one that was excavated in Hawaii from the Western Yellow Jacket, which is a pest in Hawaii.

261
00:19:25,500 --> 00:19:31,000
This nest had become perennial, and it was six feet by three feet, and it was in the ground.

262
00:19:31,880 --> 00:19:35,300
And it consisted of probably hundreds of thousands of cells and workers.

263
00:19:35,740 --> 00:19:36,940
It was just enormous.

264
00:19:37,680 --> 00:19:39,300
I've never seen anything bigger.

265
00:19:40,160 --> 00:19:43,620
But winged insects aren't the only airborne animal engineers.

266
00:19:43,621 --> 00:19:50,440
birds have been building nests for millions of years they too are hardwired to build

267
00:19:50,441 --> 00:19:53,980
that doesn't mean there's no room for improvement

268
00:19:53,981 --> 00:20:01,840
during a good year the u.s honey industry can produce more than 215 million pounds of honey

269
