We now return to Nature's Engineers on Modern Marvels.

336
00:24:08,060 --> 00:24:09,460
There's no place like home.

337
00:24:10,800 --> 00:24:14,420
Humans have been subscribing to that philosophy since we began dwelling in caves.

338
00:24:15,460 --> 00:24:19,100
Today, houses are some of the most prolific structures on the face of the planet.

339
00:24:19,101 --> 00:24:28,040
But housing tracts filled with identical structures and one-of-a-kind mansions are simply the latest examples in a long line of structural evolution.

340
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:36,940
An evolution that started thousands of years ago, when people first gathered sticks, straw, rocks, and mud to build their homes.

341
00:24:38,220 --> 00:24:42,080
Something birds figured out how to do millions of years earlier.

342
00:24:42,900 --> 00:24:44,340
Why do birds build nests?

343
00:24:45,000 --> 00:24:50,240
Birds build nests to protect their young, to shield them from adverse weather,

344
00:24:50,620 --> 00:24:51,920
to protect them from predators,

345
00:24:52,520 --> 00:24:57,000
to make sure that the young have all of the warmth that they need to develop correctly.

346
00:24:57,260 --> 00:25:00,820
Since all birds lay eggs, they don't give birth to live young.

347
00:25:01,020 --> 00:25:02,920
They need to have protection for the eggs.

348
00:25:03,160 --> 00:25:07,260
All birds may lay eggs, but the nests they build vary greatly.

349
00:25:08,260 --> 00:25:12,480
Ultimately, time and evolution have had the greatest effect on the structures birds build.

350
00:25:13,400 --> 00:25:18,280
The earlier birds, the ones that you see records of being in the fossil record from way back,

351
00:25:18,500 --> 00:25:22,660
those birds tend to build pretty simple nests constructed, say, in the sand.

352
00:25:23,380 --> 00:25:25,520
The birds that have come on the scene more recently,

353
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:29,940
those birds are the ones that build more complex nests and actually weave nests.

354
00:25:31,200 --> 00:25:35,660
North American bold eagles build their homes from materials found on the forest floor.

355
00:25:36,620 --> 00:25:42,220
High in the treetops, eagles intertwine fallen branches to form nests up to four feet wide.

356
00:25:43,200 --> 00:25:48,280
But no matter what their size, most stick-built nests share a similar characteristic.

357
00:25:48,900 --> 00:25:49,920
They're cozy inside.

358
00:25:50,620 --> 00:25:53,500
This is the nest of a house wren, which is common across North America.

359
00:25:54,040 --> 00:25:56,680
And this is built generally inside some other structure.

360
00:25:56,940 --> 00:25:58,860
You can see the way the bird has constructed this nest.

361
00:25:58,940 --> 00:26:02,820
It started out with larger fibers and branches in the bottom part of the nest

362
00:26:02,821 --> 00:26:05,340
that allows the nest to withstand elements.

363
00:26:05,660 --> 00:26:09,360
As you get closer and closer to the cup, they start using more fine fibers.

364
00:26:09,460 --> 00:26:15,520
Here you can see small grasses, and then the actual cup itself, where the eggs are, is lined with feathers.

365
00:26:16,180 --> 00:26:18,020
But not all birds are woodworkers.

366
00:26:19,200 --> 00:26:24,620
Many strive for something a little more concrete, and make mud their construction material of choice.

367
00:26:26,380 --> 00:26:31,300
Swallows build their nests high on the walls of buildings or under bridges and freeway overpasses.

368
00:26:32,320 --> 00:26:34,860
Inside, the birds lay straw and feathers for insulation.

369
00:26:35,660 --> 00:26:38,220
and to provide a soft floor for their eggs and young.

370
00:26:40,280 --> 00:26:43,200
Other birds don't need the strength of solid mud construction.

371
00:26:44,480 --> 00:26:47,840
Instead, they thrive by building small and inconspicuous nests.

372
00:26:48,580 --> 00:26:50,100
This is one of my favorite nests.

373
00:26:50,200 --> 00:26:52,200
This is the nest of an anus hummingbird,

374
00:26:52,320 --> 00:26:54,240
which is found along the Pacific coast of North America.

375
00:26:55,060 --> 00:26:58,580
And the anus hummingbird constructs its nest essentially using spider webs,

376
00:26:58,920 --> 00:27:02,780
and then it attaches lichens and other small grass and so forth

377
00:27:02,781 --> 00:27:03,900
around the edge of the nest,

378
00:27:03,901 --> 00:27:07,220
and then lines the nest with cottony material that it gets from plants.

379
00:27:07,820 --> 00:27:10,760
The lichen actually helps to conceal the nest on the branch,

380
00:27:11,500 --> 00:27:14,320
which is a camouflage that the bird relies on

381
00:27:14,321 --> 00:27:17,260
to keep other birds from finding its nest to predate its young.

382
00:27:17,680 --> 00:27:19,860
If you've ever tried to find a hummingbird nest in a tree,

383
00:27:20,420 --> 00:27:21,820
it is extremely difficult.

384
00:27:22,500 --> 00:27:26,260
Masters of disguise, structural engineers, homemakers.

385
00:27:27,020 --> 00:27:31,280
For humans, it's often easy to see creativity and intelligence in the bird world.

386
00:27:32,120 --> 00:27:33,320
It doesn't really exist.

387
00:27:33,900 --> 00:27:38,320
When it comes to birds, it's easy to believe that what we see involves more intelligence.

388
00:27:38,980 --> 00:27:43,540
And what they build looks superficially much more intelligent and much more complex.

389
00:27:43,920 --> 00:27:45,500
But in fact, it really isn't.

390
00:27:45,680 --> 00:27:51,200
The idea that they have some concept of what the nest is going to look like is a romantic fiction.

391
00:27:51,520 --> 00:27:53,160
It's been shown in lots of species of bird.

392
00:27:53,161 --> 00:27:55,860
My favorite, though, are just birds that build simple cup nests.

393
00:27:56,140 --> 00:28:01,060
If you cut a hole in the bottom, slightly larger than an egg, the birds will not repair it.

394
00:28:01,061 --> 00:28:03,820
Instead, they will just mindlessly lay their eggs one a day.

395
00:28:04,200 --> 00:28:07,240
Into this nest, the eggs will roll out the bottom,

396
00:28:07,400 --> 00:28:09,420
and the birds will just keep right on laying them.

397
00:28:10,360 --> 00:28:14,820
Still, the birds don't rely entirely on hardwired routines when constructing nests.

398
00:28:15,940 --> 00:28:18,780
Their techniques prove that their brains are designed to learn.

399
00:28:19,540 --> 00:28:24,360
Birds definitely improve in their nest-building capabilities from season to season.

400
00:28:24,361 --> 00:28:28,420
When they first start out, most bird species are not great at building nests.

401
00:28:28,421 --> 00:28:35,020
So as they practice, though, from season to season, they learn how to hold nest material.

402
00:28:35,240 --> 00:28:41,520
They learn how to pick particular plant materials that are better and will make a nest last longer.

403
00:28:42,000 --> 00:28:46,900
So usually by the second or third year of building a nest, the nests are made very, very well.

404
00:28:46,901 --> 00:28:54,860
I think we're fascinated with animals and the things they build, mainly because of the complexity

405
00:28:54,861 --> 00:28:59,980
and yet the relative ease with which the animals create these structures.

406
00:29:00,760 --> 00:29:04,080
You think about trying to build a nest yourself,

407
00:29:04,520 --> 00:29:06,900
you can realize that we would be very clumsy.

408
00:29:07,540 --> 00:29:11,420
I used to have my animal behavior classes sit around tables

409
00:29:11,421 --> 00:29:14,440
and try to make a robin's nest or an oriole nest.

410
00:29:14,620 --> 00:29:17,160
Very quickly, people threw in the towel.

411
00:29:18,320 --> 00:29:21,280
And an oriole nest seems rudimentary when compared to this,

412
00:29:21,281 --> 00:29:24,940
the ornithological equivalent of the moonshot.

413
00:29:26,700 --> 00:29:30,820
It's built by a type of African weaver called a Cassin's melimbe.

414
00:29:31,780 --> 00:29:34,280
This is the most intricate nest.

415
00:29:34,860 --> 00:29:39,480
This is one of the finest engineer work in the bird world.

416
00:29:39,980 --> 00:29:42,160
This is not just a bunch of sticks put together.

417
00:29:42,500 --> 00:29:49,240
This long tunnel in here actually protects the babies or the eggs from predators like snakes.

418
00:29:49,241 --> 00:29:50,560
There's a lot in that area.

419
00:29:50,561 --> 00:29:58,740
If a snake go in there and try to go in the tunnel, the snake is going to fall because it's too hard to go in and get the babies.

420
00:29:59,180 --> 00:30:05,760
But while some birds impress humans with their building skills, others try to impress their mates with their flair for decorating.

421
00:30:07,280 --> 00:30:14,320
The bowerbirds of Australia build bowers or mating arenas and then use a splash of color to attract females.

422
00:30:14,321 --> 00:30:19,560
The males go out and try to collect material that's of a particular color.

423
00:30:19,561 --> 00:30:21,460
In the case of satin bowerbirds, it's blue.

424
00:30:21,461 --> 00:30:27,380
So they'll collect blue feathers, blue cigarette packs, blue clothespins, whatever happens to be blue.

425
00:30:28,260 --> 00:30:30,780
It used to be thought that the males just had to scatter these out.

426
00:30:31,080 --> 00:30:37,240
But then people did experiments in which they would, while the male was away feeding, actually move one or two of the decorations.

427
00:30:37,760 --> 00:30:43,540
A male would arrive back and stare in an obviously disturbed manner at this array.

428
00:30:43,541 --> 00:30:48,360
and after a while find the two redeployed objects and put them back where they belong.

429
00:30:48,680 --> 00:30:51,680
People have actually done experiments in which they have made it impossible

430
00:30:51,681 --> 00:30:55,740
to move the objects that have been slipped to the side,

431
00:30:56,060 --> 00:31:00,280
and the males have gotten so upset they've torn down the bower and started an entirely new one.

432
00:31:01,740 --> 00:31:06,400
Experiments aside, birds and humans are increasingly sharing the same space.

433
00:31:07,440 --> 00:31:12,880
As a result, some very unnatural materials are ending up in some very natural places.

434
00:31:13,540 --> 00:31:15,220
Often to the birds detriment.

435
00:31:16,340 --> 00:31:19,240
Birds are definitely opportunists when it comes to building nests.

436
00:31:19,660 --> 00:31:24,120
They'll use natural materials, so anything that they can find, usually plant materials.

437
00:31:24,520 --> 00:31:27,920
But they'll definitely also use whatever else is available.

438
00:31:28,680 --> 00:31:33,640
Including materials that can strangle baby birds or rob the nest of life-giving warmth,

439
00:31:34,740 --> 00:31:35,560
such as yarn,

440
00:31:38,300 --> 00:31:39,500
metal wire,

441
00:31:39,501 --> 00:31:44,400
and in the case of this nest, monofilament fishing line.

442
00:31:47,260 --> 00:31:53,280
Like the birds, many creatures possess the innate desire to find shelter and protect their young.

443
00:31:54,820 --> 00:31:58,940
But some animal engineers aren't satisfied with simply building a home

444
00:31:58,941 --> 00:32:05,700
or even an apartment complex, opting instead to build an entire city.

445
00:32:05,701 --> 00:32:12,940
Some nest builders find the best construction materials right at the tips of their wings.

446
00:32:13,980 --> 00:32:18,300
The nests of the long-tailed tit may contain as many as 2,000 feathers.

447
00:32:19,780 --> 00:32:22,580
Nature's Engineers will return on Modern Marvels.

448
00:32:27,040 --> 00:32:30,780
We now return to Nature's Engineers on Modern Marvels.

449
00:32:31,740 --> 00:32:35,280
The modern skyscraper is a city unto itself.

450
00:32:35,700 --> 00:32:41,700
Builders of high-rises must consider every detail of construction before they begin.

451
00:32:41,701 --> 00:32:47,700
Engineers take into consideration the occupants' needs, such as mobility within the building,

452
00:32:47,701 --> 00:32:54,700
water, air circulation to maintain comfort, and of course a stable, properly constructed framework

453
00:32:54,701 --> 00:32:57,700
to keep it all from toppling over.

454
00:32:57,701 --> 00:33:02,700
Just like their human counterparts, animals intent on building skyward

455
00:33:02,701 --> 00:33:06,880
must design for their own comfort and safety inside their towering cities.

456
00:33:07,880 --> 00:33:12,040
Termites have to be the master champions of animal builders,

457
00:33:12,440 --> 00:33:16,480
at least compared to their size, perhaps a millimeter or two long.

458
00:33:16,740 --> 00:33:20,340
Nevertheless, the structures they'll build can be 10 or 20 feet high,

459
00:33:20,420 --> 00:33:23,340
the equivalent of a building half a mile high for us.

460
00:33:23,860 --> 00:33:26,740
Moreover, they will dig tunnels down to the water table,

461
00:33:26,741 --> 00:33:30,140
and 50 feet for a termite is more like a mile and a half,

462
00:33:30,260 --> 00:33:32,320
again, far deeper than any human well.

463
00:33:32,700 --> 00:33:38,700
Remarkably, these massive structures that populate both the African grassland and Australian outback

464
00:33:38,701 --> 00:33:44,700
are created one mouthful at a time and may take more than a decade to complete.

465
00:33:44,701 --> 00:33:51,700
The insects remove dirt from deep inside the nest and deposit the muddy saliva mixture at the surface.

466
00:33:51,701 --> 00:33:59,700
Inside the mound, the termites make use of one of humanity's greatest engineering discoveries, the arch.

467
00:33:59,701 --> 00:34:05,960
The reason for building the arches is simply to build a very porous structure, minimize the use of materials,

468
00:34:06,500 --> 00:34:11,240
but use arches because arches are, as the Romans knew, inherently strong.

469
00:34:11,520 --> 00:34:17,060
This is the way they're able to build a structure that's incredibly tall and yet doesn't have a lot of material in it.

470
00:34:17,480 --> 00:34:20,120
And its main purpose, after all, is air conditioning.

471
00:34:20,520 --> 00:34:21,220
Air conditioning?

472
00:34:22,420 --> 00:34:28,740
Incredibly, the mounds are built not only to circulate fresh air, but also keep the nest cool in the hot sun.

473
00:34:29,700 --> 00:34:39,092
Convection currents in the nest are powered by heat, generated both internally from the million-plus termites, and externally by the sun, warming the walls of the

474
00:34:39,093 --> 00:34:39,793
mound.

475
00:34:40,860 --> 00:34:47,660
The warm carbon dioxide-laden interior air rises and seeps through the porous exterior walls and upper stacks.

476
00:34:48,180 --> 00:34:51,200
Fresh oxygen-rich air replaces the escaping gas.

477
00:34:51,540 --> 00:34:56,800
It then drops into the nest, where it is further cooled by moisture, evaporating out of the mud.

478
00:34:56,801 --> 00:35:03,800
The termites are ingrained with a strong desire to build, but not all species look skyward.

479
00:35:03,801 --> 00:35:08,800
Others construct small nests out of recycled material.

480
00:35:08,801 --> 00:35:11,800
This is a colony of Pacific dampwood termites.

481
00:35:11,801 --> 00:35:16,800
You can see they're constructing, using fecal pellets, a ring-like structure here,

482
00:35:16,801 --> 00:35:19,800
which eventually they will glue to the surface of the container.

483
00:35:19,801 --> 00:35:24,800
So the sort of coarse, crumbly-looking material is actually structural material that they've been building.

484
00:35:24,801 --> 00:35:29,420
been building. Eventually they'll have the whole container filled with a matrix made

485
00:35:29,421 --> 00:35:33,260
of fecal pellets.

486
00:35:33,261 --> 00:35:36,243
Ants are another social insect that build nests.

487
00:35:36,244 --> 00:35:38,480
Like the termites, some have evolved

488
00:35:38,481 --> 00:35:44,640
to build with materials that are readily available, even if those materials are other ants.

489
00:35:44,641 --> 00:35:49,618
One of the more interesting nest architectures is with African driver ants.

490
00:35:49,619 --> 00:35:50,680
In the new world

491
00:35:50,681 --> 00:35:52,014
called army ants.

492
00:35:52,015 --> 00:35:57,900
Some of these nests can be tremendous, over 20 million ants within a single

493
00:35:57,901 --> 00:36:01,244
colony. And they're not in a permanent nest site.

494
00:36:01,245 --> 00:36:04,520
They form bivouacs that move from place to place

495
00:36:04,521 --> 00:36:05,756
to find new food.

496
00:36:05,757 --> 00:36:11,720
Within this mass of living ants that are linked together, they form their chambers

497
00:36:11,721 --> 00:36:12,689
and their tunnels.

498
00:36:12,690 --> 00:36:16,940
But some of the most sophisticated ant structures can be found under your feet.

499
00:36:16,941 --> 00:36:21,540
Many species build huge underground cities

500
00:36:21,541 --> 00:36:23,260
Just like a child's ant farm

501
00:36:23,261 --> 00:36:25,340
Only hundreds of times larger

502
00:36:25,341 --> 00:36:27,560
Using their jaws and feet

503
00:36:27,561 --> 00:36:30,120
They excavate tunnels, build bridges

504
00:36:30,121 --> 00:36:32,960
And construct intricate cells to store food

505
00:36:32,961 --> 00:36:34,480
And house their young brood

506
00:36:34,481 --> 00:36:39,140
We say that our civilization is built on the opposable thumb

507
00:36:39,141 --> 00:36:42,500
Where we can manipulate materials to build our structures

508
00:36:42,501 --> 00:36:44,120
Well in the ant society

509
00:36:44,121 --> 00:36:49,120
we might say that it's built on the mandible, the opposable jaws,

510
00:36:49,860 --> 00:36:52,320
which they use for defense.

511
00:36:52,600 --> 00:36:54,840
They use it for moving the brood around.

512
00:36:55,160 --> 00:36:57,400
They use it for excavating their nests.

513
00:36:58,320 --> 00:37:02,320
The only visible indications of the complex and often deep-reaching nests

514
00:37:02,321 --> 00:37:06,380
are their openings, which can be as small as the point of a pencil.

515
00:37:07,720 --> 00:37:11,280
And these pencil points are exactly what Professor Walter Chinkel

516
00:37:11,281 --> 00:37:18,280
of Florida State University is interested in finding.

517
00:37:24,660 --> 00:37:31,660
His goal is to understand the ants by understanding the structures they build.

518
00:37:31,661 --> 00:37:38,660
And the best tools for his work are a portable kiln and some very hot sink.

519
00:37:41,280 --> 00:37:47,960
Pretty good. Definitely hot.

520
00:37:48,500 --> 00:37:53,320
The professor uses 600 degree molten metal to cast the insides of ant colonies.

521
00:37:54,440 --> 00:38:00,200
With the metal heating, the professor and his assistant take to the field to find the ant holes.

522
00:38:00,560 --> 00:38:03,740
Okay, so these are the first two ant nests we're going to do.

523
00:38:04,400 --> 00:38:10,240
You kind of have to prepare the entrance a little because they're a little narrow right now to pour metal into accurately.

524
00:38:10,241 --> 00:38:15,060
so we're going to open these up a little bit to give us a better target for the metal.

525
00:38:15,520 --> 00:38:18,960
We try to find ant nests that are recently abandoned

526
00:38:18,961 --> 00:38:22,340
because obviously we don't really like to kill ants.

527
00:38:23,140 --> 00:38:24,600
Okay, so that's ready.

528
00:38:25,200 --> 00:38:28,740
With multiple ant colonies marked for casting, it's time to pour.

529
00:38:33,040 --> 00:38:36,980
The professor quickly carries the smoldering molten zinc to the first hole.

530
00:38:40,240 --> 00:38:42,440
Okay, uh, the far one first.

531
00:38:45,320 --> 00:38:46,020
Okay.

532
00:38:51,400 --> 00:38:52,160
Bullseye.

533
00:38:53,440 --> 00:38:56,640
Once the metal is cooled, the hard part will begin.

534
00:38:58,600 --> 00:38:59,300
Okay.

535
00:39:02,860 --> 00:39:06,120
The professor must now find the bottom of the ant hole.

536
00:39:10,240 --> 00:39:19,840
The depth and complexity of these nests can even catch the experts off guard.

537
00:39:27,040 --> 00:39:36,440
Well, looks like this one's deeper than I expected, so we're going to have to dig a pit next to it so we can stand in it and get this nest out in one piece.

538
00:39:36,441 --> 00:39:42,440
With each shovel full of sand, more of the ants' hidden world is revealed.

539
00:39:42,441 --> 00:39:49,440
The basic structure of this ant nest is a vertical tunnel with horizontal chambers coming off of it.

540
00:39:49,441 --> 00:39:54,440
And that's a very common structure for many species of ant nest.

541
00:39:54,441 --> 00:39:57,440
So what we're going to do now is I'm going to hold this nest up here,

542
00:39:57,441 --> 00:40:00,440
and Kevin's going to loosen it and I'll pull it out.

543
00:40:00,441 --> 00:40:01,440
Here we go.

544
00:40:01,441 --> 00:40:03,440
I got it. Okay.

545
00:40:03,441 --> 00:40:09,440
Okay. All right. Is that gonna...

546
00:40:09,441 --> 00:40:10,440
Look at that.

547
00:40:10,441 --> 00:40:12,440
I hope it doesn't break.

548
00:40:14,440 --> 00:40:15,440
Whoo!

549
00:40:15,441 --> 00:40:16,440
That is complete, complete.

550
00:40:16,441 --> 00:40:17,440
That is a big one.

551
00:40:17,441 --> 00:40:18,895
It looks fairly typical.

552
00:40:18,896 --> 00:40:23,440
It's got the vertical tunnel and chambers that are connected by the tunnel.

553
00:40:23,441 --> 00:40:27,440
They almost always end in a blind chamber like that.

554
00:40:27,441 --> 00:40:30,440
Okay, I'm gonna carry it to the truck, all right?

555
00:40:30,441 --> 00:40:37,580
Back on campus, the professor studies the over 100 casts he has pulled from the ground.

556
00:40:38,220 --> 00:40:43,780
What you have here is a nest cast of the Florida harvester ant, Porgonomyrmex badias.

557
00:40:44,240 --> 00:40:47,200
And you can see it has a very distinct architecture.

558
00:40:48,220 --> 00:40:52,280
The largest and most complex chambers are near the top, near the surface.

559
00:40:52,880 --> 00:40:58,620
And the chambers get smaller and less complex and further apart as you go down the nest.

560
00:40:58,621 --> 00:41:06,900
In the bottom, a third of the nests are the brood and most of the time the queen, that is the young ants, and the youngest adult workers.

561
00:41:07,720 --> 00:41:12,040
And as they get older, they gradually move upward in the nest.

562
00:41:12,340 --> 00:41:14,520
Only the oldest ones are found near the surface.

563
00:41:15,240 --> 00:41:19,460
How ants create this complex architecture is something of a mystery.

564
00:41:19,820 --> 00:41:23,180
They do it in the dark, without a blueprint and without a leader.

565
00:41:23,940 --> 00:41:29,200
Somehow, each ant knows exactly what needs to be done, how to interact with each other in the nest,

566
00:41:29,380 --> 00:41:34,500
and the result is what you see here, this rather complex and species-typical architecture.

567
00:41:35,160 --> 00:41:39,140
By working together, the ants and the cities they build thrive.

568
00:41:40,080 --> 00:41:42,340
But not every bug depends on the colony.

569
00:41:44,620 --> 00:41:48,460
Spiders produce and weave some of the finest homes in the animal world

570
00:41:48,461 --> 00:41:52,660
and will happily eat any insect unfortunate enough to join them.

571
00:41:53,180 --> 00:41:58,620
Some African termite colonies have no shortage of builders.

572
00:41:59,000 --> 00:42:02,420
Their queens can produce up to 30,000 eggs a day.

573
00:42:03,420 --> 00:42:06,340
Nature's Engineers will return on Modern Marvels.

574
00:42:10,340 --> 00:42:14,180
We now return to Nature's Engineers on Modern Marvels.

575
00:42:14,900 --> 00:42:16,640
The bulletproof vest.

576
00:42:17,180 --> 00:42:19,700
One of law enforcement's best defensive tools.

577
00:42:20,700 --> 00:42:22,560
The secret to its success.

578
00:42:23,180 --> 00:42:29,180
is an amazing synthetic material known as Kevlar.

579
00:42:29,840 --> 00:42:35,140
Many times stronger than steel, the material is light and can be woven together like fabric.

580
00:42:36,500 --> 00:42:40,120
Every year, millions of dollars are spent on superfiber research,

581
00:42:40,440 --> 00:42:47,840
as scientists and engineers look for the seemingly impossible combination of light, strong, and flexible.

582
00:42:47,841 --> 00:42:53,600
But many entomologists know that they don't need to look past their backyard,

583
00:42:54,080 --> 00:42:59,580
because when it comes to making new supermaterials, eight-limbed arachnids have a definite leg up.

584
00:43:00,140 --> 00:43:04,120
While spider silk is almost as strong as high tensile steel,

585
00:43:04,540 --> 00:43:10,840
what really sets spider apart from all other biological and man-made materials is its incredible toughness.

586
00:43:11,740 --> 00:43:15,720
And toughness is the amount of energy that the material can absorb before failure.

587
00:43:15,721 --> 00:43:22,720
Toughness allows webs to stretch and not break, like this one snaring a bat in mid-flight.

588
00:43:22,721 --> 00:43:29,720
Not only does it have a high tensile strength, but it's also highly extensible.

589
00:43:29,721 --> 00:43:36,720
So dragline soap, for example, can stretch about 25% beyond its original length.

590
00:43:36,721 --> 00:43:46,840
In contrast, some man-made fibers such as high tensile steel or Kevlar are very stiff and they can't be stretched very far before they break.

591
00:43:47,940 --> 00:43:54,220
This machine is a nano tensile tester that we use to look at the mechanical properties of spider silks.

592
00:43:54,221 --> 00:43:59,720
by mounting a sample of a single spider silk thread onto the machine.

593
00:43:59,721 --> 00:44:03,200
The machine can then gradually pull that thread

594
00:44:03,201 --> 00:44:07,620
and measure the amount of force that's needed to pull the thread until it breaks.

595
00:44:08,180 --> 00:44:11,780
This allows us to test how strong the fiber is,

596
00:44:12,020 --> 00:44:15,120
how stretchy the fiber is, and how tough it is.

597
00:44:15,680 --> 00:44:19,060
Spider silk may eventually lead to products stronger than steel,

598
00:44:19,520 --> 00:44:21,600
but far lighter and much more flexible.

599
00:44:21,601 --> 00:44:28,840
With all its potential applications, it's sometimes easy to forget that this material emanates from the end of a spider.

600
00:44:30,460 --> 00:44:36,540
The animal physically pulls the final fibrous product from small spinnerets at the rear of its abdomen.

601
00:44:37,020 --> 00:44:39,840
But the silk production itself starts deep within the creature.

602
00:44:40,960 --> 00:44:51,200
If you could look inside a spider's abdomen, you would find anywhere from just a few to perhaps even thousands and thousands of tiny individual silk glands.

603
00:44:51,600 --> 00:44:54,840
From these silk lands, the silk travels down a narrow duct,

604
00:44:55,140 --> 00:44:56,880
and as it travels down the narrow duct,

605
00:44:57,300 --> 00:45:01,700
the liquid silk is transformed into a dry fiber.

606
00:45:02,080 --> 00:45:06,160
So by the time the silk exits the spider through the spigots on the spinnerets,

607
00:45:06,380 --> 00:45:08,180
we have a solid fiber.

608
00:45:08,760 --> 00:45:10,880
As impressive as the silk production is,

609
00:45:10,881 --> 00:45:13,940
what the spider does with the material is just as amazing.

610
00:45:15,220 --> 00:45:19,540
The best-known spider constructions are the common circular webs, called orb webs.

611
00:45:19,541 --> 00:45:22,380
but their building process is anything but common.

612
00:45:23,420 --> 00:45:27,320
Now an orb web is made up of several types of architectural elements.

613
00:45:27,820 --> 00:45:30,580
The first thing the spider does is construct the frame

614
00:45:30,581 --> 00:45:32,360
and that's made from one type of silk.

615
00:45:32,780 --> 00:45:37,480
Then the next thing the spider does is lay down radii from a center point.

616
00:45:37,660 --> 00:45:42,020
So she lays out radii and that's using the same kind of silk that was used in the frame.

617
00:45:42,380 --> 00:45:45,140
After the frame and radii are constructed,

618
00:45:45,141 --> 00:45:47,460
the spider prepares the capture spiral.

619
00:45:47,461 --> 00:45:50,780
And the capture spiral is a mixture of two types of silk.

620
00:45:50,980 --> 00:45:58,220
One is a stretchy fiber, and the other one is a gluey droplet that gets laid down over this stretchy piece of silk.

621
00:45:58,520 --> 00:46:02,580
And these gluey droplets allow insects to actually get stuck to the web.

622
00:46:03,460 --> 00:46:06,800
Every type of orb-weaving spider spins a different web pattern.

623
00:46:07,400 --> 00:46:10,320
But the final product is always a lethal trap.

624
00:46:11,220 --> 00:46:14,620
The webs are able to snare insects many times bigger than the spider.

625
00:46:14,621 --> 00:46:20,520
and on occasion, the unforgiving strands not only capture bats, but also small birds.

626
00:46:21,500 --> 00:46:26,460
Despite the silk's incredible toughness and the engineering involved in creating the webs,

627
00:46:27,120 --> 00:46:31,400
at the end of the day, the spiders often do something abhorrent to most engineers.

628
00:46:31,980 --> 00:46:36,480
They destroy their masterpieces to build them again the following day.

629
00:46:37,440 --> 00:46:42,660
The silk is largely made up of protein, and protein is a very valuable resource to an animal.

630
00:46:42,661 --> 00:46:49,180
So a typical orb-weaving spider will actually recycle their silk from day to day.

631
00:46:49,181 --> 00:46:56,080
If you ever watch a spider in your yard take a web down, she actually very carefully bundles all the silk up into a little ball,

632
00:46:56,520 --> 00:47:03,040
and then she'll actually consume that ball and recycle the amino acids from that web into tomorrow's web.

633
00:47:03,760 --> 00:47:05,980
The orb-weaver webs are some of the best known.

634
00:47:06,920 --> 00:47:08,620
They certainly aren't the only ones out there.

635
00:47:08,621 --> 00:47:12,920
Some spiders weave silken parachutes that help them take to the wind.

636
00:47:13,700 --> 00:47:17,300
Others build cozy subterranean homes.

637
00:47:19,260 --> 00:47:24,360
While some of the most infamous web spinners make what appears to be a mess.

638
00:47:24,760 --> 00:47:25,580
This is a black widow.

639
00:47:25,940 --> 00:47:27,960
The web that black widows make is very tough,

640
00:47:28,160 --> 00:47:30,820
and even though it's messy looking and not well engineered,

641
00:47:31,280 --> 00:47:36,580
it works perfectly for the kind of close quarters that black widows need their webs to be housed in.

642
00:47:36,581 --> 00:47:38,460
You can see how tough this is.

643
00:47:38,461 --> 00:47:42,840
I'm actually pulling the sticks inside with the web that she's got still attached.

644
00:47:45,460 --> 00:47:48,720
Spiders share a common thread with all animal engineers.

645
00:47:50,900 --> 00:47:53,940
These specialized creatures depend for their survival

646
00:47:53,941 --> 00:47:58,120
on their mysterious desire to manipulate the world around them.

647
00:47:59,700 --> 00:48:04,920
The fascination to me is that the animals create these incredibly complex

648
00:48:04,921 --> 00:48:09,140
and yet highly adapted structure without any prior experience.

649
00:48:09,420 --> 00:48:12,740
Just the idea that they know to make them and they know how to make them

650
00:48:12,741 --> 00:48:17,200
and that each species seems to make a different one is impressive compared to humans

651
00:48:17,201 --> 00:48:20,700
who seem to have no clue how to make anything without being trained.

652
00:48:21,180 --> 00:48:26,340
Our ability to learn and capacity for abstract thought may separate us from the animals.

653
00:48:27,120 --> 00:48:32,360
But no matter how massive our creations grow or how advanced our construction materials become,

654
00:48:32,361 --> 00:48:38,620
animals around the world remind us that we aren't the first builders but simply the latest

655
00:48:38,621 --> 00:48:41,580
in a long line of nature's engineers

